---

There is a kind of sadness that arrives without a reason. Nothing has gone wrong. Nobody has died. The work is fine, the people are fine, and still something in the chest sits lower than it did yesterday. We have more or less agreed, as a culture, that this is a malfunction. Something to be named, tracked, optimised away.

I want to say a quiet word in its defense.

First, a distinction, because it matters. Depression is an illness. It flattens everything, takes the colour out of food and music and people, and it deserves treatment, not poetry. Melancholy is something else. Melancholy is heavy but alive. Food still tastes like food. Music sounds better, if anything. It is sadness with the lights on.

## What it is for

The painters I keep on the walls of this site understood this. Friedrich's figures stand at the edge of the sea, small, coats pulled tight, facing an enormous indifference — and they are not miserable. They are paying attention. That is what melancholy is, I think. A mood in which the true size of things becomes visible. Most days we move too fast to see it. Then one evening the speed drops away, and there it is: everything you love is temporary, everyone you know is improvising, and you are one small lamp in a very large night.

That is not a distortion of reality. That is reality. The cheerfulness we perform most of the day is the distortion — useful, social, necessary, but a distortion all the same.

## The refusal to be fixed

What bothers me is not that we prefer happiness. Of course we do. What bothers me is the assumption that a low mood is always a problem with a solution — a walk, a supplement, a gratitude journal, a better morning routine. Sometimes it is. But sometimes the sadness is simply proportionate. You lose things. You disappoint people. Time passes and does not come back. A person who never feels the weight of that is not healthy; they are just not looking.

So when the mood arrives now, I try not to reach for the toolbox straight away. I make tea. I put on the music that agrees with it. I let it say what it came to say, which is usually some version of: *this matters, and it will not last.*

It always leaves on its own. And it tends to leave something behind — a little more patience, a little more tenderness toward other people, who are, after all, carrying the same weather and mostly not mentioning it.

Melancholy without despair. It is on the list of things I like, and it earned its place.
